// TEST: Closed-Form Domain: A Negative-Remainder Generator Converts Alike In Scalar And Cell Code
// The bench's input generator shape -- integer rounds ending in `% 2^31`, then
// one `Float64(h)` -- with a NEGATIVE seed, so every remainder is negative
// (Blade's `%` truncates toward zero in every lane). `gen_scalar` fills `g`
// from a plain range map: scalar code, the plain conversion. `gen_cell` is
// the same body called from a closed-form domain kernel: cell code, the
// vectorizable split. They must agree bit for bit at every cell (`miss`),
// and `g` pins the values a C-semantics reference gives.
let static n = 37
let static w = 3
type Nx = Idx<n>

static struct Band {
    i: Int64<min=0, max=n-1>,
    j: Int64<min=0, max=n-1>
} where abs(i - j) <= w

function gen_scalar(k: Int64, seed: Int64) -> Float64 = {
    let h = ((k + seed) * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648
    Float64(h) / 2147483648.0
}

function gen_cell(k: Int64, seed: Int64) -> Float64 = {
    let h = ((k + seed) * 1103515245 + 12345) % 2147483648
    Float64(h) / 2147483648.0
}

let g = method_for(range<Nx>) <@> lambda(k) -> gen_scalar(Int64(k), -1000003) |> compute
let negatives = reduce(method_for(range<Nx>) <@> lambda(k) -> if g(k) < 0.0 then 1 else 0, (+))
// EXPECT: negatives = 37
let g_sum = reduce(g, (+))
// EXPECT: g_sum = -18.9984497670084
let miss = reduce(method_for(range<Band>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> if gen_cell(i, -1000003) == g((i : Nx)) then 0 else 1, (+))
// EXPECT: miss = 0
let quad = reduce(method_for(range<Band>) <@> lambda(i, j) -> g((i : Nx)) * g((j : Nx)), (+))
// EXPECT: quad = 65.9761971186334
